      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.06,
            0.086,
            0.115,
            0.102
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.054,
            0.109,
            0.196,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms do not show any colour. The colours depicted in the diagrams are for illustrative purposes only."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.082,
            0.89,
            0.134
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a phenomenon known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "radioactivity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This showed that atoms must be composed of smaller particles, proving that they were not indivisible as previously believed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.135,
            0.891,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In 1897, J. J. Thomson studied the conduction of electric current through gases at a very low pressure. He used a glass tube with two electrodes and applied a high voltage. He observed rays moving from the cathode (negative electrode) to the anode (positive electrode) (Fig. 8.1). These were called cathode rays. By studying these cathode rays in electric and magnetic fields, he concluded that they are streams of negatively charged particles, with a much smaller mass than atoms. These particles, later called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "electrons",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", were emitted from atoms, indicating that atoms are composed of smaller subatomic components."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.313,
            0.244,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.065,
            0.34,
            0.163,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.065,
                0.34,
                0.163,
                0.443
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.342,
            0.294,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "J. J. Thomson’s",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " most significant discovery was that of the electron, the first"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.065,
            0.45,
            0.288,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "subatomic particle to be identified and a part of every atom. He received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1906 for his studies of the electrical conductivity of gases. This research led him to discover electrons. As the head of the famous Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, he guided and inspired many scientists, including Ernest Rutherford."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.295,
            0.847,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.41,
                0.295,
                0.847,
                0.511
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.471,
                0.514,
                0.783,
                0.531
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.1: A line diagram of cathode ray tube"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.54,
            0.89,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It was found that the nature of cathode rays was independent of the material of the cathode and the gas filled in the cathode ray tube. It showed that electrons are a fundamental component of all atoms, present in every element. The charge of an electron "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( - 1 . 6 0 2 \\times \\bar { 1 0 ^ { - 1 9 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " C) is taken as –1 as a matter of convention and convenience."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.651,
            0.716,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.2.1 Thomson’s model of an atom"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.673,
            0.89,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When J. J. Thomson discovered tiny negatively charged particles called electrons, he faced a puzzle — atoms are neutral, so where is the positive charge present? To solve this, Thomson proposed the atom to be a sphere of positive charge with electrons distributed throughout it (Fig. 8.2). This model was compared to a pudding with plums embedded in it, called the plum pudding model. A more familiar picture would be"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.05,
            0.72,
            0.315,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.05,
                0.72,
                0.315,
                0.87
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.081,
                0.878,
                0.288,
                0.907
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.2: Thomson’s model of an atom"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.334,
            0.792,
            0.693,
            0.928
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "that of a watermelon (Fig. 8.3), where the red pulp represents the positively charged matter, and the seeds represent electrons distributed throughout the atom. This simple picture, though later replaced, was the first genuine attempt to describe how the atom’s positive and negative charges stay balanced."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
